<p>Businesses in Phase 4 — those with turnover between RM1 million and RM5 million, technically required to begin e-invoicing on 1 January 2026 — have been given meaningfully more breathing room than originally planned. LHDN has extended the relaxation period from its earlier 2026 end date out to 31 December 2027. Full enforcement, including real penalties under Section 120 of the Income Tax Act, now doesn''t begin until 1 January 2028.</p>
<p>Worth being precise about what "relaxation" actually permits, since it isn''t a free pass: during this window, businesses can issue consolidated e-invoices and use more flexible product/service descriptions, and won''t face Section 120 penalties for non-compliance. What it doesn''t do is remove the underlying obligation to register on MyInvois and show genuine, ongoing effort toward compliance — LHDN''s guidance is explicit that the relaxation period is for businesses actively transitioning, not a reason to defer starting entirely.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> a full two extra years before real enforcement is a genuine gift of time, but it''s worth using deliberately rather than banking it as an excuse to wait until late 2027 to start. Businesses that register on MyInvois and begin issuing consolidated e-invoices now will have a far smoother transition to full individual-transaction compliance by 2028 than those who treat the extended deadline as license to do nothing until it''s nearly up.</p>');
